If BOD5 and SS are found to double in the incoming sewage, high-concentration organic wastewater may flow in or a large amount of manure may enter the plant. This will increase the processing load.
The ratio of BOD5:N:P for sewage treatment with activated sludge should be maintained above 100:5:1, which can generally be achieved in urban sewage plants. Some industrial wastewater cannot reach this ratio, and nutrients must be added to the wastewater.
Dissolved oxygen in the effluent of a sewage treatment plant is beneficial to the water environment. If possible, some dissolved oxygen should be allowed in the effluent.
The sludge load has a great influence on the treatment effect, sludge growth and oxygen demand, which must be carefully controlled. Generally speaking, the sludge load is 0.2～0.5kg(BOD5)/(kg.d, controlled at 0.3kg(BOD5)/”kg(MLSS).d”.
The daily burden of BOD5 per unit volume of the aeration tank is called volume load kg(BOD5)/(m3.d). The volumetric load indicates the economics of constructing the aeration tank.
Sludge age is the ratio of the total amount of activated sludge working in the aeration tank to the remaining sludge discharged every day, and the unit is d. When the operation is stable, it can be understood as the average residence time of activated sludge in aeration.
The volatile suspended solids concentration of the mixed liquid refers to the weight of the organic matter in the suspended solids of the mixed liquid (usually measured by the burning loss at 600°C), so some people think that it can more accurately represent the number of activated sludge microorganisms than MLSS. However, MLVSS also includes inactive and non-degradable organics, and it is not ideal for measuring MLSS.
