Plastic, glass or latex… regardless of the electrical insulation, its role is the same: to act as a barrier to electric current. Indispensable to any electrical installation, it performs many functions on any network, whether it spans hundreds of kilometers or covers your entire home.
The electrical insulator is defined by any component or organ whose purpose is to prevent the passage of an electric current between two conductive parts. Also called dielectric material, it has few free charges and therefore has zero conductivity, hence its insulating characteristics.
• its electrical resistance. This value, which is mentioned in ohms (Ω), makes it possible to know the ability of the material to oppose the electric current;
These atoms consist of a nucleus, around which gravitate electrons. There are several energy levels, called the band theory, which allow us to know in which energy value the electrons of a solid are located:
In these insulating materials, the conductivity is (almost) zero, and these electrons do not move between the bands and the atoms. They therefore act as barriers to electric current.
The insulating capacity of certain materials can be broken if their breakdown voltage (or dielectric strength) is reached. This is what happens during electric arcs in particular (in this case, the insulation is air).
Limit losses Channeling energy so that it does not go outwards: this is another use of electrical insulators.
• 78% when driving on the lines. The electricity heats the cable and undergoes energy losses (this is the Joule effect);
