• the insulating materials, for their part, simply fulfill a “bulwark” function against the flow of electrons. What nuance between the two?
Dielectric materials are therefore widely used to store an electric field, an essential function for capacitors. These small electronic compounds are also composed of three main elements, namely two electrodes separated by a dielectric material.
• the air contains electrically neutral molecules, in particular nitrogen and oxygen, which makes it partly insulating. On the other hand, once the breakdown voltage has been reached, an ionization phenomenon is triggered and generates an electric arc.
• dry wood is also part of the family of natural electrical insulators. Be careful because the wood can become a conductor if it becomes wet;
How do you know if a material is an insulator?
• if the current flows, it is not insulating;
Today, most of the insulators used in the industrial, domestic environment, or even electrical networks are artificial. Among them are:
• glass, which was used until the mid-1970s for glass insulators for power lines;
• rubber, used to make electrically insulating mats to prevent professionals, such as welders, from being electrocuted at their workstations;
• polyimide is a very thin material (thickness of 1 mm) but which offers great resistance to electricity. It is applied in certain fields such as aerospace or in the military environment.
• its insulating properties;
But this characteristic leads to another: resistance to heat.
An electrical insulator blocks electricity, but not only because electricity produces heat, and the material must also be able to cope with it. This ability to resist heat is established by the IEC 60085 standard which lists insulation in 8 thermal classes:
Like any product, quality is an essential criterion if you want an effective insulator. For example, glass was widely used in the 19th century, but showed its limits to the detriment of other better quality insulators, and at the best price.
